-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Singular Matrices:** The inversion of $\mathbf{\Sigma}_i + \mathbf{\Sigma}_j$ or $\mathbf{\Sigma}$ in the Bhattacharya distance may fail if covariances are singular. A guard (e.g., adding small diagonal noise) is required [G].
-*   **Leaf Node Initialization:** Raw optimized Gaussians are too numerous for direct leaf nodes. They must first be grouped into local clusters [S1]. The method for this clustering is not detailed in the sources [G].
-*   **Descriptor Learning:** The plane descriptors $\mathbf{z}$ and normals $\mathbf{n}$ are learned via auxiliary losses (normal loss, SAM mask lifting) during training, not just constructed [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Computational Expense:** Raw Gaussian primitives cannot be used as leaf nodes due to the large number of Gaussians in a scene; they must be pre-clustered into local clusters [S1].
+*   **Descriptor Learning:** The plane descriptors $z$ and normal vectors $n$ are learned during optimization using 2D normal maps (via Omnidata) and 2D SAM masks, respectively [S1]. If these are not available, the merging criteria cannot be fully applied.
+*   **Precision:** The Bhattacharya distance involves matrix inversion and logarithms of determinants. Degenerate covariances (singular matrices) will cause numerical instability [G].
